ick tock is  its jewish users now just cannot escape  the issue um in a way that's really  kind of not okay people who have no  connection to israel have never lived in  israel have never  sort of expressed support for the  actions of israel's government  and are doing a video about something  totally unrelated  we'll just find all of their reactions  and responses are free palestine  um just as we don't think it's  acceptable to hold say  a british muslim accountable for what  the state of saudi arabia does  there really is problems inherent in  that kind of  response and we are seeing quite a lot  of that on techsoc  seth you're in jerusalem can you give us  an update on what's happening right now  yeah i think there is an attempt now to  achieve a ceasefire between  israel and hamas but there has also been  so far i think on by day 11 a total of 4  000 rockets fired and  there was an attack on a bus this  morning a military bus near the gaza  strip by an anti-tank missile  then we've had sirens around the gaza  strip but i think up into ashkelon so  it does appear that you know hamas will  try to  continue firing rockets and achieve  attacks right up until whatever  ceasefire is broken  so that they can claim that they kind of  won and i think that's what we'll see  um there has been a relatively let's say  quiet eight or ten hours and i don't  think there's been a lot of airstrikes  by israel as well so  it remains to be seen if by friday uh  the conflict may end  seth what we're seeing on social media  is a growing but not necessarily new  phenomenon  can you talk to us about the history of  social media in middle east conflicts  i know it was used notably in the 2011  arab spring for example  yeah i mean i think that social media  certainly helped to transform the middle  east in terms of giving lots of people  um you know a decade a half ago a lot  more of a voice average people  i think though there was a change you  know after the arab spring in which you  