d and that  sort of  you know networked informal kind of  thing  sort of helps pull people in it helps  sort of build a community it helps get a  bit of  credo people like to feel knowing  and so it sort of does appeal  to people it's you know it's it's good  cause it's a good protest cause  um and sort of young adults in every  generation have always wanted those  um and of course it does attach to a  real  serious political movement um it's just  often the way people social introduces  people to it  can be quite strange and it should be  said there is quite a lot of  outright misinformation um on both  on actually pro-israel and pro-palestine  accounts you know people reusing old  videos people kind of knowingly  misleading um including some people tied  to ministries  so it's it's really a mess out there  seth finally um to follow on from  james's point  and point you were making before when  you were saying that actually in many  ways in the past social media has been  used as a  force for good um how do you weigh it up  now  with these kinds of videos going around  with the disinformation  do you think social media has been a  force for good when it comes to  political matters  um i don't know if it's necessarily a  force for good but i mean it's just it  is it's  it's a force it's a medium right i mean  i don't know if radio  or tv or necessarily or print media have  always been a force for good i mean it's  a nature of  it's the nature of a platform so i just  think it exists i think that it can do  good i think when you have a conflict  you know you obviously tend to then  emphasize all the worst  attributes of things because obviously  you know the  the worst scenes of children being  killed or what have you are going to be  posted even if they're fake but it  doesn't matter that's the kind of thing  that's going to be  passed around but i think look after the  abraham accords last year when there was  peace between israel and some of the  gulf states  you 