ht to both get the military to  persuade the military and their  press editors to give them permission to  report  was was essentially very much central to  their experience  but as i write i think the fact that  for several years they had to use their  own ingenuity  martha galcon for instance riding  stairway on a hospital ship  in order to cover the d-day landings  because  women were not allowed it often meant  that they had a very different color and  a very different heartbeat  to their stories clarissa to compare and  contrast what is it like today  i mean it's so you know it's so  interesting hearing due to talk about  that because you see  how much progress has been made um an  extraordinary amount  there are a huge number of women like  myself on the front lines doing this  kind of reporting  um and i think for the most part we are  treated with equality and sometimes i  even think we have an advantage  in some conservative societies for  example we're able to get to places  that our male colleagues can't get to  and we're able to sit down  uh with women in their part of the house  and and tell their stories as well which  is  a real privilege and something that's  been absolutely lacking  uh from the sort of broader coverage of  war particularly  in conservative muslim societies for for  many many years  so i do think a lot has changed i don't  think it's always  completely changed um i do think that  there  are still elements of misogyny and  sexism and i do think that  you face um unique threats as well  potentially in conflict zones  as a woman but i am heartened to hear  um how how far i think we've come and  i'm also i think like many  other female war correspondents  profoundly inspired by the legacy of  those women who due to the describes  i was so interested when you said that  it was  easier often to get the stories of women  from these conflict zones as a woman but  also i think my women in world war ii  often found it was easy to get stories  