y and to report on  everything  for the chicago tribune you know risking  threats of interrogation  and worse that for her that was a hugely  moral  crusade um i think for martha gelhorn  too writing about the war from the point  of view of the suffering victims  the injustice of the the fascist powers  that that for her her  her thought that if she could change  public opinion if she could change the  world  that would sort of that justified every  single word that she wrote  um for some of the others some for  instance claire hollingworth  it was more a sense of  competitiveness the absolute  adrenaline of the scoop getting to the  headlines first  that essentially drove her and for  someone like virginia coles who  um was a kind of uh  hilariously rogue operator because for  years she would  turn up in trenches in high-heeled shoes  and lipstick  for her it was as much curiosity she was  just  thrilled to be seeing the world thrilled  to be expanding her own horizons  so within that essential ambitiousness  they  they had different motives but  interestingly to  although they would give each other  loyal support and friendship  when their paths crossed they didn't  care to be seen  as a sort of professional sisterhood  they they because they were they were  such a minority out in the field  and having to battle so hard to be taken  as seriously as men  they were quite reluctant to uh  to be presented as a kind of suffering  minority  to and they so they they looked they  looked out for each other but they were  very much  solo operators clarissa last question to  you i'd like to hear what you think on  this what qualities are needed to be a  war reporter  i think the first quality that's needed  honestly is curiosity  you have to be really curious you have  to be a good listener  because you have to be willing to hear  things that you're uncomfortable with  that challenge your own value systems  and you have to be willing to give  everyone a fair shake  and hear 